0,Tsimihety dialect - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsimihety_dialect,"Tsimihety dialect belongs to the Austronesian language family. It is part of Northern Malagasy subgroup along with Northern Sakalava, Bushi, Northern Betsimisaraka and Antakarana. [5][6]","Malagasy, Tsimihety",Austronesian
1,Music of Madagascar - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Madagascar,"Music in Madagascar tends toward major keys and diatonic scales, [6] although coastal music makes frequent use of minor keys, most likely due to early Arab influences at coastal ports of call. [7] Malagasy music has served a wide range of social, spiritual and mundane functions across the centuries.","Malagasy, Tsimihety",Austronesian
2,Tsimihety dialect — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/tsimihety_dialect,"The Tsimihety dialect (ISO 639-3: xmw) is a northern variety of the Malagasy language, an Austronesian tongue originating from Southeast Asian migrations to Madagascar around the 7th century CE, spoken primarily by the Tsimihety ethnic group in the central-northern region of the island, including areas like Mandritsara and the Sofia province. [1] With approximately 1.6 million speakers as of ...","Malagasy, Tsimihety",Austronesian
3,Tsimihety dialect explained,https://everything.explained.today/Tsimihety_dialect/,"Classification Tsimihety dialect belongs to the Austronesian language family. It is part of Northern Malagasy subgroup along with Northern Sakalava, Bushi, Northern Betsimisaraka and Antakarana. [5][6]","Malagasy, Tsimihety",Austronesian
4,"Malagasy, Tsimihety language - Global Recordings Network",https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/xmw,"Malagasy, Tsimihety language and dialect information. Audio Bible stories and lessons. Download free evangelism resources, MP3s, audio bible study tools, language/dialect information.","Malagasy, Tsimihety",Austronesian
5,Music of Madagascar — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Music_of_Madagascar,"The music of Madagascar comprises the diverse traditional and contemporary styles performed by the Malagasy people, reflecting the island's settlement by Austronesian migrants from Southeast Asia around the 5th to 10th centuries CE and subsequent Bantu arrivals from East Africa, which produced a distinctive synthesis of melodic chordophones and rhythmic percussion. [1][2] Traditional Malagasy ...","Malagasy, Tsimihety",Austronesian
